        "summary": "hypothesis=Bill structural observations on this admission worth cohort-watching: (1) NO PHARMACY LINE on a 6-day surgical-stay bill. Possible reasons: pharmacy bundled into surgeon/OT charges, hospital-absorbed pharmacy, or pharmacy dispensed but not enclosed in folder (Part B 'Pharmacy bills' checkbox is ticked despite no enclosure — inconsistent filing). Compare companion folder (case_eeb12f48bd43) which had Rs.284 pharmacy line for a 3-day stay. (2) ROLLING PRE-OP PATTERN — only Rs.200 minimal 4-test panel (Hb/RBG/BG/BT-CT) on 21-May, no repeat of comprehensive HIV/Hep/PT-INR/urine panel done 18 days earlier (3-May, for companion admission). Hospital appears to re-use comprehensive workup if within ~3 weeks. Cost-efficient pattern, clinically reasonable. (3) ROOM-CATEGORY FHPL-LETTER DISCREPANCY — FHPL letter says 'General Ward' on this admission, actual bill says 'SINGEL ROOM A/C-201'. Companion folder had no such discrepancy (Single A/C consistent across letter and bill). Either FHPL letter has stale-default fields on Oriental product flows, or payer failed to apply room-differential deduction. The Rs.5,430 'MOU discount (do not collect)' on this case is suspiciously round at exactly 15.0% of bill — could be a flat hospital-MOU rate rather than an actual room-differential calc. (4) RATE STRUCTURE DIFFERENCE vs companion folder — per-day rates differ (Room Rs.1000 vs Rs.2000, Nursing Rs.300 vs Rs.800, Consultant Rs.1200 vs Rs.1000, Surgeon Rs.15,000 vs Rs.7,000). Same patient, same room category (Single A/C), same hospital, ~3 weeks apart — but rate structure differs by 50-100% on several lines. Suggests packaged-rate billing rather than line-item-tariff billing, with the package varying by procedure complexity. All four items above feed payer_profile_store and cohort-level rate-card discovery.; tier=3",
