        "summary": "hypothesis=Paramount/National Insurance Co. corporate GMC deduction model on this case carries ALL FOUR deduction kinds simultaneously: (1) policy-level co-pay (Rs.16,512 = 15% per policy T&C, collectible from patient), (2) hospital-side discount (Rs.17,400, absorbed by hospital), (3) tariff-excess on professional fees (Rs.9,600, absorbed by hospital with explicit 'DO NOT COLLECT FROM PATIENT'), (4) itemised non-admissibles across investigation, miscellaneous, and consumables buckets (Rs.57,616, collectible from patient). Total Rs.94,128 of insured-side or hospital-side adjustments against a Rs.194,698 bill — 48.3% of the bill is not paid by the insurer at the authorised rate. This is structurally distinct from the three prior models observed in cohort: FHPL bucketed tariff-restriction (all hospital-absorbed); Bajaj retail itemised non-admissibles (all patient-collectible, no copay/discount); Bajaj corporate hybrid (non-admissibles + tariff-excess, no copay/discount). Hypothesis: the 'corporate GMC with explicit copay condition' policy profile drives the most complex deduction structure, with the hospital exposed on TWO axes (discount + tariff-excess) and the patient exposed on TWO axes (copay + non-admissibles). The recoverable lever profile is different from prior cases: NOT pure tariff renegotiation (case 1) NOR pure patient-collection (case 2 retail) NOR a hybrid (case 3) — instead (a) audit the patient collection of Rs.74,128 (substantial; if uncollected, hospital absorbs); (b) audit whether the discount Rs.17,400 is required by an MoU or hospital-discretionary; (c) tariff renegotiation only on the Rs.9,600 professional-fees-excess line. Cry-wolf guarded; does NOT feed tier1_recoverable. Testable against future Paramount/NIC cases in cohort.; tier=3",
