        "summary": "hypothesis=Bajaj direct cases use an itemised-particular deduction model with explicit 'collectible-from-patient' vs 'tariff-excess-do-not-collect' separation; FHPL/NIA cases use a bucketed 'Restricted to agreed tariff' deduction on a single 'Others' row absorbed entirely by hospital. On this Bajaj retail paediatric case: Rs.3,839 disallowed (7.3% of bill), ALL collectible from patient (non-admissible items: monitor Rs.3,500 + specific pharmacy consumables Rs.339); ZERO tariff-excess absorption. The recoverable lever profile is therefore opposite to the FHPL/NIA hypothesis: less about renegotiating tariff (since Bajaj already accepted hospital tariff on this case), more about (a) reducing reliance on Bajaj's non-payable items list during care (or pre-clearing them with parent), and (b) parent-payment collection-rate audit at discharge.; tier=3",
