        "summary": "hypothesis=First cohort instance of cross-case policy_ref equality. This case shares the same Apitoria Pharma NIA/FHPL group-GMP policy (policy_ref pol_71e1a46e399c) as case_f90295b5e3c2 (ingest order 1). Two different employees of Apitoria Pharma (different primary_insured_member_refs) have spouses delivering LSCS at Jyothi Nursing Home within ~6 days of each other (comparator case admitted 16-May, this case 22-May). Cohort pattern question worth raising: are there other Apitoria employees on the same group GMP with claims at Jyothi in the 33-folder cohort? If yes, this corp may be a meaningful Jyothi catchment (employees recommend hospital to colleagues; or hospital has a direct billing relationship with the corp). Both LSCS cases on this same policy show the same DEDUCTION SHAPE — single-line 'restricted to agreed tariff' with full hospital absorption. Both have zero patient liability. Both used Single A/C rooms. ABSORPTION RATE DIFFERS: this case absorbs Rs.24,488 (41.2% of Rs.59,488 bill) vs the comparator case's Rs.16,853 (29.6% of Rs.56,853 bill). The bills differ only slightly (5%); the rate-card differential explains most of the absorption gap (this case has higher per-day rates on Room, Consultant, Nursing — see e008 rate_structure_vs_comparator_lscs_note). Tier-3 question for cohort: is the NIA/FHPL LSCS tariff cap roughly Rs.35,000-40,000 at this hospital? With the comparator case authorised at Rs.40,000 and this case at Rs.35,000 — both on the same policy and same procedure — the variance Rs.5,000 may indicate (a) a tariff range rather than a flat cap, (b) procedure-complexity variation (primigravida emergency LSCS vs repeat LSCS), or (c) negotiated tariffs with floor and ceiling.; tier=3",
